  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry through the week, with temperatures\nrunning near to just above normal through the end of the week \ninto next weekend.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates an amplified upper pattern,\nwith deep upper troughing digging across the west coast, an\nupper-level ridge in place from the west-central Gulf coast to \nthe Great Lakes and an upper trough sliding offshore. At the \nsurface, ~1030 mb high pressure is centered just west of the \nlocal area, bringing seasonably mild temperatures and \nlight/variable winds.\n\nNot quite as cool tonight, though efficient decoupling under a \nweak pressure gradient will still support some upper 30s in the \nnormally colder spots of rural south-central VA. Lows will range\nmainly in the upper 30s to lower 40s inland, and mid to upper \n40s along the coast. Some patchy fog possible late tonight over\nsouth central VA/interior NE NC.\n\nA weak, dry front is forecast to drop south through the area \ntomorrow, veering winds to the N-NE with a modest speed increase\nalong the coast. High pressure becomes centered north of the \nlocal area Tuesday night through Wednesday, maintaining near-\nnormal temperatures. The high then anchors off the southern New \nEngland to Mid-Atlantic coast Wednesday night and Thursday while\nlow pressure remains suppressed well south near the Bahamas. \nWith the high offshore, SSW winds trend highs above normal late\nin the week, with highs warming well into the 70s Fri/Sat.\n\nRemaining dry into the weekend, as upper ridging builds over \nthe region. A northern stream shortwave traversing central \nCanada will flatten the upper pattern Friday into the weekend. \nHalloween looks dry, pleasant, and mild across the region, \nthough some increasing clouds will be possible ahead of a weak\ncold front, and a more unsettled pattern which is looming for \nthe first half of next week. Trends from 12z/26 probabilistic \nmodels do show increasing potential for light rain as early as \nlate Sunday into Monday, more likely on Monday. However, this \nprogressive regime (at least at first) is unlikely to deliver \nany substantial drought-busting rain through early November, \nwith both GEFS and EPS showing a very low probability for 24-hr \nQPF AOA 0.50\" through at least early next week. Climatologically,\ndespite ongoing precipitation deficits across much of the area,\nand even with no rain prospects on the horizon through Halloween,\nnone of our climate sites will rank among the top 10 driest \nOctobers on record, owing to several past Octobers with monthly \nrainfall below 0.50\".",
